-- title:
--   Integral points of a Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient as Γ'-orbits of adic points
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $r$. The arithmetic data consist of: a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible element $\pi$ (`hπ`) with $\mathcal O$ complete for the $(\pi)$-adic topology (`hcomplete`), residue ring of cardinality $r$ (`hres`) and $(r)=(\pi)$ in $\mathcal O$ (`hunr`), together with a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   The unramified layer consists of a characteristic-zero domain $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ over $\mathcal O$ and an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$ of it, subject to: completeness of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), maximality of that ideal (`hOnr_max`), the condition that every element of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), the condition that every monic polynomial over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and the congruence $\mathrm{Fr}(x)\equiv x^{r}\pmod{\pi}$ for all $x$ (`hFr`). A monoid homomorphism $v\!\det : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)\to\mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) is given, characterised by `hvdet`: $v\!\det(g)=n$ if and only if $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   The group-theoretic data consist of a group $G$, a homomorphism $\sigma : G\to \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$, a subgroup $\Gamma\le G$ containing an element $z$ with $\sigma z$ a scalar matrix and $v\!\det(\sigma z)=2$ (`hcent`) and an element $w$ with $v\!\det(\sigma w)=1$ (`hodd`), and a subgroup $\Gamma'$ whose members are exactly the elements of $\Gamma$ with $v\!\det(\sigma\,\cdot)$ even (`hΓ'`). Further, $\rho : G\to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ is the homomorphism induced by $\sigma$ (`hρ`), and the image $\rho(\Gamma')$ acts on the set of vertices of the lattice tree of $K_0^2$ over $\mathcal O$ (homothety classes of full $\mathcal O$-lattices) with finite vertex stabilisers (`hdisc`) and with finitely many orbits, in the form of a finite set $S$ of vertices meeting every orbit (`hcocpt`).
--
--   Finally, $f : \mathcal X\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is a proper flat morphism of schemes, and $\Theta$ is a family of maps which, for each $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, sends a pair consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to B$ and a Deligne datum over $B$ (an assignment to each full lattice $M\subseteq K_0^2$ of a $B$-submodule of $B\otimes_{\mathcal O} M$ with invertible quotient, compatible with inclusions of lattices and with homothety, and satisfying the nondegeneracy condition at every prime of $B$) to an element of $(\mathtt{Scheme.nilpPoints}\ f).\mathrm{obj}\ B$, that is a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} B\to\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$. Four hypotheses govern $\Theta$: `hΘnat`, naturality in $B$ along $\mathcal O$-algebra maps; `hΘinv`, invariance: for $\gamma\in\Gamma$ and pairs $x,x'$ over $B$ related by `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` for $\sigma\gamma$ (namely $x'$ has first component $x$'s first component precomposed with $\mathrm{Fr}^{-v\!\det(\sigma\gamma)}$, and $x'$'s Deligne datum is the pullback of $x$'s along $(\sigma\gamma)^{-1}$), one has $\Theta(x')=\Theta(x)$; `hΘfib`, the fibre description over algebraically closed fields $k$ over $\mathcal O$ with $\pi$ nilpotent and a fixed $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to k$, asserting both that every $k$-point of $\mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$ is of the form $\Theta(\psi,P)$ and that $\Theta(\psi,P)=\Theta(\psi,P')$ holds if and only if $(\psi,P)$ and $(\psi,P')$ are related by `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` for some $\sigma\gamma$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma$; and `hΘuniv`, universality: for every scheme $T$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every family $\rho'$ satisfying the same naturality and $\Gamma$-invariance conditions with values in the $T$-points, there is a natural family $u$ of maps from the nilpotent points of $f$ to those of $T$ with $u\circ\Theta=\rho'$, and any natural family $u'$ with $u'\circ\Theta=\rho'$ agrees with $u$.
--
--   The geometric base is an algebraically closed field $C$ over $K_0$, valued in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$ and complete, together with a commutative ring $R$ that is an $\mathcal O$-algebra and a $C$-subalgebra in the compatible sense (scalar towers $\mathcal O\to R\to C$ and $\mathcal O\to K_0\to C$), a pseudo-uniformizer $\varpi$ of $K_0$ in $C$ (an element of $K_0$ whose valuation in $C$ lies strictly between $0$ and $1$ and scales the valuations of all nonzero elements of $K_0$), and the hypothesis `hF` that $(\pi,\varpi,R)$ is an adic frame: $\pi$ is irreducible, $R\to C$ is injective with image exactly the elements of $C$ of valuation $\le 1$, $R$ is $\pi$-adically complete, the elements of $K_0$ of valuation $\le 1$ in $C$ are exactly those coming from $\mathcal O$, the image of $K_0$ in $C$ is closed, and $\pi$ and $\varpi$ have the same image in $C$. Lastly $\psi_0 : O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to R$ is an $\mathcal O$-algebra map.
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exists a map $\Phi$ from `AdicPoint K₀ π R`, the type of families $(x_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$ of Deligne data over $R/(\pi^{n+1})$ compatible under the transition maps, to the set of morphisms $p:\operatorname{Spec} R\to\mathcal X$ with $p$ followed by $f$ equal to the structure morphism $\operatorname{Spec} R\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, such that the following four assertions hold.
--
--   First, for every adic point $x$ and every $n$, the reduction $\operatorname{Spec}(R/(\pi^{n+1}))\to\operatorname{Spec} R$ followed by $\Phi(x)$ equals the morphism underlying $\Theta$ evaluated on the pair consisting of $\psi_0$ reduced modulo $\pi^{n+1}$ and the $n$-th component $x.\mathrm{pt}\,n$.
--
--   Second, $\Phi$ is surjective.
--
--   Third, for adic points $x,x'$ one has $\Phi(x)=\Phi(x')$ if and only if there is $\gamma\in\Gamma'$ with $x'=x.\mathrm{act}(\sigma\gamma)$, the levelwise pullback of the Deligne data along $\sigma\gamma$.
--
--   Fourth, the restriction map along $R\to C$, sending $p$ to $\operatorname{Spec} C\to\operatorname{Spec} R$ followed by $p$, is injective on the set of morphisms $\operatorname{Spec} R\to\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, and every morphism $\operatorname{Spec} C\to\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is in its image. Thus $\Gamma'$-orbits of adic points over $R$ correspond bijectively to the $R$-points of $\mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$, and these in turn to the $C$-points.
--
--   This is the integral-points step of the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation: the quotient of Drinfeld's formal upper half plane by $\Gamma'$ is identified, on points with values in the valuation ring $R$ of a complete algebraically closed field $C$, with the $R$-points of the proper flat $\mathcal O$-scheme $\mathcal X$ represented by $\Theta$, and by the valuative criterion with the $C$-points of its generic fibre. It feeds the identification of the function field of the generic fibre of the Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient.
